<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<!-- Brought over from jsk-enshu/robot-programming (jedy/jedy_description).

     Two changes from upstream, both recorded here because a diff against
     upstream will show them:

     1. Meshes moved to meshes/gz/. This package already had a jedy mesh set
        with the SAME filenames and DIFFERENT content: upstream's have the link
        transform baked in (every visual/collision <origin> here is zero), this
        package's do not. Mixing the two sets draws a robot that parses,
        articulates, and is assembled wrong. The subdirectory is what keeps
        them apart.
     2. The <ros2_control> block and the gz_ros2_control plugin below are now
        conditional on gz_control, so the same kinematics can be driven by a
        real backend. Expanded with no arguments this file is still exactly the
        Gazebo model it was; jedy_bringup expands it with gz_control:=false and
        supplies its own <ros2_control>. -->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro" xmlns:drake="http://drake.mit.edu" name="jedy">
  <!-- true = keep the gz_ros2_control block below (upstream behaviour).
       false = emit kinematics only, for a caller that adds its own. -->
  <xacro:arg name="gz_control" default="true"/>
  <!-- Head camera in Gazebo: wide | d405 (see the rgbd_camera sensor). -->
  <xacro:arg name="camera_model" default="wide"/>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       Joint passive dynamics: what holds a joint whose servo is OFF.
       (GOAL phase 4, 2026-11-07. This URDF is the ONE place these numbers
       live; Gazebo reads <dynamics> directly, scikit-robot's urdf_to_mjcf /
       urdf_to_usd copy it into MuJoCo / Isaac. No simulator has its own.)

       Where each number goes:
       * <dynamics friction> = tau_f [N m], the static (Coulomb) friction
         at the joint: a load below it is held still with the torque off, a
         load above it back-drives the gear train.
       * <dynamics damping> = b [N m s/rad], viscous friction at the joint.
       * armature = rotor inertia x gear ratio^2 [kg m^2], the inertia the
         joint feels from the spinning motor. URDF has no tag for it; it is
         written the way Drake reads it, <drake:rotor_inertia> and
         <drake:gear_ratio> in the joint's <transmission><actuator>
         (https://drake.mit.edu/doxygen_cxx/group__multibody__parsing.html),
         with the standard <mechanicalReduction> next to it. Decision log:
         jedy/jedy_description/README_dynamics.md.

       THE SERVO: KRS-3302 ICS, ASSUMED (UNVERIFIED). Nothing in this repo or
       upstream names the model. The URDF's velocity="6.55" rad/s is exactly
       KRS-3302 ICS's 0.16 s/60 deg at 7.4 V (60 deg / 0.16 s = 6.545 rad/s);
       no other ICS servo has that speed. effort="0.48" does NOT match it
       (6.7 kgf cm = 0.657 N m); kept as upstream wrote it.
       Product page: https://kondo-robot.com/robospot/html/products/detail.php?product_id=1852
         stall torque      6.7 kgf cm = 0.657 N m @ 7.4 V (5.5 kgf cm @ 6.0 V)   datasheet
         no-load speed     0.16 s/60 deg = 6.545 rad/s @ 7.4 V                    datasheet
         gear ratio        N = 262.92                                             datasheet
         gear type         plastic spur gear train                                datasheet (material); 平歯 (spur) is how KRS servos are built, stage count 不明
         motor             cored DC motor, square can; model 不明                  datasheet
         rotor inertia     J_r 不明. Estimated 1e-8 kg m^2 (0.1 g cm^2, the order of a ~15 mm cored motor)   UNVERIFIED
         forward/back-drive efficiency  不明. Estimated eta = 0.6 both ways (multi-stage plastic spur)  UNVERIFIED
         torque constant / winding resistance  不明
         backlash          不明 (not on the datasheet)
         OFF mode          ICS "free" (position 0 command). Whether the driver floats
                           the motor (coast) or shorts it (brake) is 不明: the ICS 3.5
                           manual says neither (https://kondo-robot.com/w/wp-content/uploads/ICS3_5SoftwareManual_V1English.pdf).
                           Coast is assumed here.

       Derived (the same for every KRS-3302 joint until measured):
         motor stall torque   tau_ms = 0.657 / (N eta) = 0.657 / (262.92 x 0.6) = 4.16e-3 N m
         motor no-load friction tau_m0 = 0.07 x tau_ms = 2.9e-4 N m
                              (0.07 = no-load / stall current of a small cored DC motor, typical, UNVERIFIED)
         tau_f = N tau_m0 / eta_back = 262.92 x 2.9e-4 / 0.6 = 0.13 N m        ESTIMATE, UNVERIFIED
         b (coast)  = mechanical viscous loss only; small. 0.001 N m s/rad      ESTIMATE, UNVERIFIED
           (if OFF turned out to be a brake, b = stall / no-load speed
            = 0.657 / 6.545 = 0.10 N m s/rad, and the arm would fall ~100x
            slower: the identification in HUMAN_CHECKLIST.md tells them apart)
         armature = J_r N^2 = 1e-8 x 262.92^2 = 6.9e-4 kg m^2                   ESTIMATE, UNVERIFIED

       Each joint below says where ITS numbers come from. To replace an
       estimate with a measurement: HUMAN_CHECKLIST.md "サーボ OFF の静止摩擦の
       同定" and jedy_skrobot's identify_friction (it rewrites the
       <dynamics> line and the "source:" line of that joint here).
       ================================================================== -->
  <link name="base_link"/>
  <link name="real_base_link">
